  <section id="why-different">
    <h2>Why rivers are different from lakes and the sea</h2>
    <p>
      On flat water a mistake usually costs you a swim. On a river the water is moving, so the same mistake can carry you toward a hazard you did not choose. Flow also increases quickly after rain, turning a gentle paddle into a fast, cold, debris-filled one within hours. Most beginner problems come from not reading the river, not from the paddling itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hazards">
    <h2>The hazards that matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weirs.</strong> A weir can create a stopper, a recirculating wave at its base that holds boats and swimmers. Many look harmless from upstream. If you see a horizon line across the river, get out and portage, using signed paths where they exist.</li>
      <li><strong>Strainers.</strong> Fallen trees and branches let water through but not people or boats, and they can pin you against them. Paddle away from them, do not go under or through, and if you are swept toward one, try to get on top.</li>
      <li><strong>Cold water.</strong> Water below about 15°C can cause cold water shock, which can cause involuntary gasping and makes self-rescue far harder. Dress for immersion, not for air temperature.</li>
      <li><strong>High water.</strong> A river in spate is faster, colder and carries more debris. If the level is above what you have paddled before, choose another day.</li>
      <li><strong>Overhanging branches, bridges and other users.</strong> Look ahead, communicate with your group and give anglers and swimmers plenty of room.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="grades">
    <h2>How river grades work</h2>
    <p>
      Whitewater is graded on the International Scale of River Difficulty from Grade I to Grade VI. Grade I is moving water with few obstacles, Grade II has small rapids and clear channels, and the grades rise through Grade III and IV, where rapids become powerful and require skilled boat control and rescue ability, up to Grade VI, which is extremely dangerous.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beginners should stay on flat water and, once they have had instruction, easy Grade I and II water, and only with experienced paddlers. Grades are not fixed. The same rapid may be Grade II at low water and a much more serious feature when the river is high, so always check the current level and read recent trip reports. Paddle UK and local clubs publish river guides and, for some rivers, level gauges.
    </p>
  </section>

