  <section id="kit">
    <h2>What to wear and carry</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Buoyancy aid.</strong> Properly fitted and worn at all times. A paddle sports buoyancy aid lets you move your arms; a bulky life jacket does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Insulation.</strong> A wetsuit or drysuit in cooler months, and spare layers in a dry bag. Cotton is a poor choice when wet.</li>
      <li><strong>Helmet and footwear.</strong> A helmet if there are rocks, weirs or low branches, and shoes with a secure fit and grip for portaging.</li>
      <li><strong>Rescue and communication.</strong> A throw line you know how to use, a whistle, a first aid kit and a phone in a waterproof case. Know your location so you can describe it to emergency services.</li>
      <li><strong>Trip plan.</strong> Tell someone your route and your expected finish time, and never paddle alone as a beginner.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="inflatables">
    <h2>Inflatable kayaks on rivers</h2>
    <p>
      Inflatables are forgiving and easy to transport, which makes them popular with beginners. They are also lighter and catch the wind more, may turn more slowly in current, and can be punctured by sharp rocks or branches. Treat an inflatable on a river with the same respect as any other boat: wear the same kit, avoid weirs and strainers and check it for leaks before you launch. Pay attention to the manufacturer's stated limits for the kind of water it is designed for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2>Decisions that keep you safe</h2>
    <p>
      Good paddlers are not fearless; they are good at deciding not to. Get out and walk around anything you cannot see the bottom of. Do not follow a more experienced friend down something you have not assessed. Turn back if the weather, level or your group's energy changes. A day of instruction from a Paddle UK coach is worth more than any amount of reading, and many clubs offer taster sessions.
    </p>
